    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Evidence for cyclic loading during opening-mode fracture propagation in rock masses is abundant. The surface textures on joint faces include nested arcuate ridges, referred to as rib marks, conchoidal structures, annular structure or hesitation lines (Hodgson,1961; Dennis, 1972; Kulander et al., 1979; Pollard and Aydin, 1988). These suggest cyclic loading or straining in geological history and are interpreted as marking the fracture front during temporary arrest of propagation. Because the arrest lines apparently are not spatially correlated with heterogeneities in rock properties, temporal changes in loading are often invoked to explain their origin.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
